=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router6
diagnosis_text:
The affected subsystem is the iptables FORWARD chain on router6. Three DROP rules are configured to match all traffic arriving inbound on eth2 (the router's only WAN uplink toward the rest of the network via router3 at 10.50.1.1):

- Rule 2: DROP all TCP packets arriving on eth2
- Rule 3: DROP all UDP packets arriving on eth2
- Rule 4: DROP all ICMP packets arriving on eth2 (confirmed active: 8 packets / 672 bytes hit)

The only exception is a narrowly-scoped ACCEPT rule allowing TCP/80 from 172.16.10.0/24 to 172.18.51.1, which is useless for general connectivity.

The mechanism: traffic outbound from the mswitch2 site (172.18.50.0/24 via router6 eth0; 172.18.51.0/24 via router6 eth1) can still leave via eth2 since the DROP rules match only -i eth2 (incoming). However, all return traffic — replies from external hosts, ICMP echo replies, TCP SYN-ACK, UDP responses — arrives on eth2 and is silently dropped before being forwarded. Additionally, any connection attempts initiated from external sites toward hosts in the mswitch2 area are also blocked on ingress at eth2. The result is complete bidirectional isolation for all three affected subnets: switch9, switch10 (pc15–pc18), and switch11 (laptop4–laptop5).

The root cause is an overly broad stateless firewall policy applied to eth2 on router6 that drops all inbound forwarded traffic, with no conntrack state exception for established or related flows.

Corrective fix: On router6, remove the three DROP rules from the FORWARD chain (`iptables -D FORWARD` for rules 4, 3, 2 in reverse order). If inbound filtering is required, replace them with a stateful rule: `iptables -A FORWARD -i eth2 -m state --state ESTABLISHED,RELATED -j ACCEPT` followed by a DROP default, so legitimate return traffic passes while unsolicited inbound connections are still blocked.
=== END ===